#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524506} {:answer/id #uuid "8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524605} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524650} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524441} :answer-field/id #uuid "717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796" :question/math-academy-id "q-107463" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4726f486-296b-54df-b7de-616f90a2a430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524505} {:answer/id #uuid "8de0ee3e-9434-591e-863f-19dc03481eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524606} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524659} {:answer/id #uuid "cafdcf0f-5e0a-5f8f-978e-3e3f2f8dc403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524715} {:answer/id #uuid "fc809e37-d2bf-588c-ad7b-ff6395a75916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+5){e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524659} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7337e6-4d8a-5b6d-9fd4-37daba6415d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b27b93f8-5f19-566f-95e3-f0a0b21ec684" :question/math-academy-id "q-11817" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={e}^{2x+5},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=2x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfd52e-1fc1-51eb-85e5-32d9d3dc6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313486} {:answer/id #uuid "f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524810} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd73eaf-0df8-5718-b843-1cd77b6090a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8593de99-5055-5455-bd33-e38ccd6c9851" :question/math-academy-id "q-340496" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=8x+{e}^{-3x+5}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{-3x+5}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+{e}^{-3x+5}) \\\\  & =8-3{e}^{-3x+5} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{e^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{e^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524710} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e1ae92-136f-5d68-8353-b8a4c9e485ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7798a8be-b1f8-549d-9dbb-a2ddea411006" :question/math-academy-id "q-340954" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{7}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-14{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-14{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-14(1)^{6}{e}^{-2(1)^{7}} \\\\  & =-14{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524839} :answer-field/id #uuid "d83b75c0-fe56-5390-8126-bd377ed6af9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70b1c8a4-0636-5508-ab80-4e4e15ec81e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340970" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3(0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-30\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b1c38d-bd47-595a-85a4-aaa20457bc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327383} {:answer/id #uuid "87871190-a65e-5c1e-8282-1faf7bde4a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8-6{x}^{2}){e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328101} {:answer/id #uuid "b8909401-18d1-5019-a6f0-ca794f50ba31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328540} {:answer/id #uuid "c387f8bc-a855-544f-9080-010895d03bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} {:answer/id #uuid "e092d66a-dbb8-5e12-ab82-47da6c4607df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c387f8bc-a855-544f-9080-010895d03bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} :answer-field/id #uuid "025f7489-d455-5ba5-a862-9edaba9cd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97112097-e97d-59a1-a70d-99c7a4daf89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48629" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=8-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
